WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.460
Double Dragon 3, The Sacred Stones.

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:09.560
Only one man knows the real story behind Marion's disappearance, and he's dead.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:33.100
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:34.900
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:36.600
And I'm Joe.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:39.440
Double Dragon 3.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:43.660
There's a 2 and a 3 going on in there.

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:45.160
I'm fine with it.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.560
Double Dragon 1 and 2, Essential Games.

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:50.260
Single Dragon 2, The Third.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:54.520
Yeah, do we have strong feelings about this franchise?

00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:02.160
We've put both games on the Essential Games list, and we haven't voted to take them off, but we don't talk about Double Dragon that often.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:03.560
No, we don't.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:08.540
And it's something that I have to admit, it's been a very busy week.

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:12.660
I really wanted to go back and play the first two, but I only ended up getting through the third one.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:19.820
Because I want to remind myself, where do these fall on my best games of the NES?

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:44.160
I think we don't talk about them too much because they were so competent, and they feel so much like even modern beat-em-ups that we just don't even think of them in the same realm as some of the games we're playing, which very often don't feel like they're from this century, which they're not.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.720
So, maybe that's why.

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:52.880
That's a good point, Sean, that beat-em-ups, when they're good, are also kind of like turn your brain off.

00:01:53.060 --> 00:02:02.020
Yeah. You know, you just keep doing punching and kicking, and it just keeps working, and it just feels good to keep clearing enemies and moving forward.

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:15.680
It's not the kind of thing of, like, a super satisfying platformer where, like, you kept missing the one jump or the RPG where you had to grind it out and you remember, like, the memorable story or something like that.

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:33.560
They do have stories, and this does pick up where Double Dragon 2 ended, but it's not the kind of thing where it's like, that's why I came here to play, was, like, I have to finish out the story like you would maybe in, you know, any Final Fantasy game.

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:36.760
Like, you most likely are there for the story first and foremost.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:49.780
Yeah, I don't think I've ever, I mean, aside from maybe some games for this podcast, I don't think I've ever beaten a beat-em-up because you don't really need to.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:55.320
Like, you get your fix, you usually don't have save points, and you move on.

00:02:56.020 --> 00:03:08.320
Yeah, if anything, maybe I beat one of the TMNT games when I was a kid, but yeah, it was, I played beat-em-ups a lot, but yeah, it's just, you just, you play, you play with a friend, you have fun, and then you're like, alright, shut it off.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:11.120
Like, you don't save it or anything, you're just like, you don't care.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:17.520
And Double Dragon 3 picks up one year after Billy and Jimmy defeated the Shadow Warriors.

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:21.780
Marion, who's been kidnapped times before, kidnapped again.

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:26.880
Uh, the only witness to that, as mentioned in the back of the box, is dead, that's Brett.

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:29.440
Uh, he dies before revealing anything.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:38.520
So, a fortune teller named Haruko shows up and says that the kidnappers are searching for the three sacred stones of power.

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:42.500
But my question is, what would Marion have to do with that?

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:44.560
I think that she killed her. Like, she's just a regular person.

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:49.300
However, how often has she been kidnapped?

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:50.940
Yeah, this is the third time.

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:52.340
This is the third time she's been kidnapped.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:04:00.180
The only reason that we know she's been kidnapped is the dying breaths of the guy on the floor in the first screen.

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:03.340
I think she just wants to get away from Billy and Jimmy.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:08.540
Maybe she's working with them and just getting kidnapped.

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:09.900
She's like a double agent.

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:14.420
When she's actually with the enemy and you think she's being held, she's actually working with them.

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:16.520
And like, when she's with you, that's when she's with the enemy.

00:04:16.520 --> 00:04:19.400
They're trying to lure you to find these stones for them.

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:20.860
Making all this up right now.

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:23.520
People just don't get kidnapped three times.

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:24.760
That's all I'm saying.

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:30.060
So, you're saying it's kind of like the Princess Peach theory where, like, she wants to be kidnapped by Bowser.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:45.900
Yes. But that makes a lot more sense because it's just like a Mario thing and there's not even lip service to the idea that whatever's happening in a Mario game is a real thing.

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:48.980
Whereas this seems to be taking place in the real world.

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:50.440
So, different.

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:53.580
Now, the Lee brothers, their name's Billy and Jimmy.

00:04:54.580 --> 00:05:02.880
But maybe that's not true because in Double Dragon 3, if you're playing two-player on the intro screen, it says,

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:07.520
A year has passed since Bimmy and Jimmy defeated the Shadow Warriors.

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:09.440
An obvious typo.

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:10.560
A funny one at that.

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:16.780
And did wind up getting referenced in later Double Dragon games as like, okay, okay, we're in on it too now.

00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:20.460
But there's something funny about their names being Bimmy and Jimmy instead.

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:29.660
It's also just funny that it's like, it feels like it's such a blatant typo that it feels like the last second they're like, shit, we didn't do a title screen.

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:30.980
They typed it out real quick and shipped it.

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:32.540
Like, nobody read it again.

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:35.280
It's the first screen you see in the game.

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:40.480
And there's like one of the main characters' name is spelled wrong.

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:45.720
I don't know. I mean, I have a friend from high school named William and we called him Bimmy.

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:47.800
Oh, that's great. Yeah.

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:49.140
Maybe it's not a typo.

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:50.780
Maybe all the Billys are typos.

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:52.400
Every time it says Billy, that's the typo.

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:56.360
Or maybe this was just like Bimmy normalization for your friend.

00:05:56.600 --> 00:05:58.940
Like, they were just trying to make it like, yeah, no, no, it's fine.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:05:59.960
Some people can be named Bimmy.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:03.040
Do you think Jimmy's full name is Jillium?

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:07.700
Yeah. That is awesome.

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:22.780
All right. And so as the Lee brothers and two additional fighters, you will travel through the United States, China, Japan, Italy, and Egypt in search of these sacred stones of power.

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:28.640
However, in the original arcade version of this game, which came first, though, they weren't sacred stones.

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:30.320
It was the Rosetta Stone.

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:37.440
And that just feels like a weird thing to change because the Rosetta Stone is like a known thing.

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:42.960
And I guess it would make sense about like ending up in Egypt if you have to get the Rosetta Stone.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:45.520
But instead, they made it about the sacred stones.

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:53.360
And we will definitely get more into that arcade version because there's some baggage behind that one.

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:56.420
But we'll save that and continue talking about the NES release.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:58.740
We're in February of 1991.

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:00.800
It's developed by Technos Japan.

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:11.680
And apparently, according to the Internet, it was the seventh best selling console game that month in the U.S., which I'll be honest, doesn't sound that high.

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:12.760
That's a lot of qualifiers.

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:19.580
Right, right. Like in the United States specifically and only seventh, like there was only like 15 games coming out that month.

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:21.620
So it was just kind of like middling, you know.

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:23.760
So that happens.

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:29.940
Apparently, there's a massive overproduction by Acclaim, who's doing the publishing for Technos Japan.

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:38.260
And there were 500,000 or more unsold copies just sitting in warehouses after that initial month.

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:43.520
That seems like a real operations problem there.

00:07:44.060 --> 00:07:50.760
Yeah. And it seems like they anticipated a lot of sales, too, because surely Double Dragon 2 would have indicated that.

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:58.840
And I don't think word of mouth was what it was now, like that you would have found out if a game was good or bad.

00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:08.820
But I will say just to like get into it, like Double Dragon 3 is initially like for me was a bit of a head scratcher.

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:12.940
And I did have to go back to one and two and be like, wait, are these games good?

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:14.140
Like, were they like this?

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:25.800
Because this game changed enough things in a confusing way that I wasn't sure if Double Dragon was always this hard and this frustrating.

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:27.500
That was what I was going to say.

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:30.380
This feels much harder than the others.

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:38.900
I don't remember it being so punishing that as soon as you die at any point in the game, you have to start the game over.

00:08:40.060 --> 00:08:41.860
Was that the case before?

00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:44.120
No, you had lives in the other games.

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:49.420
And I can't understand removing lives from this game entirely.

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:51.480
Like that is nuts.

00:08:51.480 --> 00:09:00.960
But was the idea that they were trying, like this is the third game, it should be harder, like it should feel like progressively harder and they just kind of did it in a cheap way or something?

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:08.640
Because yeah, that is like unforgiving and like it's always like the opposite of your characters getting more powerful from game to game, like power creep.

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:11.520
It's like power drain, power leak.

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:13.560
I don't know. But you also like.

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:14.360
I like power leak, yeah.

00:09:14.740 --> 00:09:17.220
Yeah, I kind of, I like that too, just coined it.

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:27.300
But I also just feel the game is, I don't remember, Mike, you can tell me if the first two games were like this, frustratingly slow in the way you move.

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:31.220
You're trudging. It's like you're walking, I'm like underwater the whole time.

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:36.900
Like that makes, like I feel like the whole time I'm playing, I'm pressing the button, the directional buttons really hard.

00:09:37.220 --> 00:09:41.420
Because like subconsciously, I'm like, go a little faster as people are attacking you.

00:09:41.500 --> 00:09:42.780
Well, you got a double tap.

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:52.440
Double tap. Well, yeah, you can double tap, but like it still feels stiff and trudging and like, you know, then you run and you can't like stop out of that.

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:56.660
But just the whole game feels, it doesn't feel smooth, I guess.

00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:06.400
Yes. So smooth is the issue because Double Dragon 3 looks a lot like Double Dragon 2, like graphically and how the character, you were asking about movement.

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:09.640
Like you do move at the same speed, you are the same size and everything.

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:15.880
But something happened with the actual combat of your character specifically.

00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:19.060
The other character, the enemies, they're doing just fine.

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:21.880
They're capable and they seem to always be able to attack.

00:10:21.880 --> 00:10:33.180
Like there's something slow about your movement and like the hip box of your punches and kicks that just don't always connect and found me kind of like air punching.

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:37.020
Like if I was in my dreams, you know, and I'm like, oh, I'm going to give it to this guy.

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:41.600
Like that happened a lot more in this one than it did in Double Dragon 2, which I did go back to.

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:51.700
So I just feel like something happened here and there was a big change that in Double Dragon 2, the original Double Dragon had this where there was dedicated punch and kick buttons.

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:55.760
Double Dragon 2 lost that, but Double Dragon 3 goes back to it.

00:10:55.760 --> 00:11:13.300
And I don't think that like that alone can be the issue, but there's just something about the timing of your attacks that feels off and winds up making it so that the enemies land a lot more of their hits on you, especially because you're dealing with multiple of them at a time in two different directions.

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:15.300
Yeah, and you're right about that.

00:11:15.400 --> 00:11:20.720
I hadn't even like thought about it as the hit boxes, but that's the huge problem, like especially your punch.

00:11:20.920 --> 00:11:28.680
Like it just feels like you have to be you have to be so close to them that that you need to have gotten hit once before before you're able to punch them.

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:31.880
And, you know, not literally, but it does feel like that a lot of times.

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:43.660
And like that that hit box thing combined with like the fact that you're there's like the feedback doesn't seem like as as immediate when you press the button.

00:11:43.660 --> 00:11:50.160
And then also the fact that every enemy, even the lowest enemies, you have to knock them down like five times to kill them.

00:11:50.280 --> 00:11:51.700
It just feels almost futile.

00:11:51.820 --> 00:11:54.340
Like every fight feels like a slog.

00:11:54.960 --> 00:12:12.060
Yeah, I don't know if it's because the the first and second game didn't have like this super move thing, but I got into a lot of situations like, you know, I was playing the game normally for the first three or four times that I died.

00:12:12.220 --> 00:12:18.220
But then I just ended up spamming like that, like spinning the spinning kick.

00:12:18.980 --> 00:12:23.620
Yeah. Or I guess maybe it's a more more Chun-Li kick that.

00:12:24.460 --> 00:12:28.300
It stopped being a beat him up and I was just cheesing it.

00:12:28.540 --> 00:12:31.980
And I never really thought to do something like that in the other game.

00:12:32.140 --> 00:12:35.400
So I think that that that speaks a lot.

00:12:35.940 --> 00:12:47.680
Yeah, I that's because, you know, and we were joking on the TMNT two episode that like you basically just wind up jump kicking everything because it covers enough ground and it just makes it this way.

00:12:47.820 --> 00:12:48.940
The enemies get hit back.

00:12:49.120 --> 00:12:52.140
And then this way you get when they get back up, you can just jump kick again.

00:12:52.140 --> 00:13:05.700
And that wound up being a similar theme here in Double Dragon three where it's like the punch and the kick maybe have such a limited range that you are just better off doing that spin kick in the air or the I mean, this is really cool.

00:13:05.800 --> 00:13:11.440
This is a cool one where you like jump on top of them and grab them by their hair and then like throw them down.

00:13:11.440 --> 00:13:21.760
Like those animations are cool and everything, but you wind up just doing the special moves because they deal more damage because they have more range and you very seldom just do the beat them up part.

00:13:22.500 --> 00:13:25.700
And that one that you're describing that grabbing them by their hair and flipping them.

00:13:26.060 --> 00:13:27.400
That's what I did, Sean.

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:34.380
Like I the instead of the spinning one, I was constantly flipping these people over to one, get them away from me.

00:13:34.560 --> 00:13:36.900
And two, because it was just like a move I could do.

00:13:36.900 --> 00:13:41.880
So it was like the whole game was me just like when someone goes at me, I throw them to the left side of the screen.

00:13:42.020 --> 00:13:43.220
I throw the other guy to the right side of the screen.

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:53.440
By that time, the other guy's coming back at the room, the left side is just throwing these people back and forth as much as I possibly can flipping around, you know, which was maybe fun at the very beginning.

00:13:53.440 --> 00:13:56.720
But it got very repetitive and tedious very fast.

00:13:57.360 --> 00:14:07.980
Yeah, that like again, just the spin kick was the only way that I could be the the first boss against the chin man chin man.

00:14:08.520 --> 00:14:15.580
I've got a I've got a trick a tip for you against the first two bosses is just use the nunchucks.

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:18.860
I usually waste that early.

00:14:19.160 --> 00:14:24.260
But yeah, yeah, I after I like died like on the boss the first time I had to start the game over.

00:14:24.260 --> 00:14:29.160
It's like I'm just saving the nunchucks until the boss and it was like, yeah, the first boss I could just beat him.

00:14:29.500 --> 00:14:32.740
One, two, three, four, five hits with one nunchucks.

00:14:32.780 --> 00:14:33.840
He's down. He doesn't do anything.

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:39.740
And chin who that is his actual name.

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:42.360
Chin say may say my.

00:14:43.020 --> 00:14:45.820
He is the first boss that you take down or not.

00:14:45.960 --> 00:14:47.040
He's in the second stage.

00:14:47.320 --> 00:14:51.340
But when you take him down, he joins your party.

00:14:51.340 --> 00:14:55.580
And so he's one of the new playable characters previously just Billy and Jimmy.

00:14:55.740 --> 00:14:59.680
Now you have chin and Yagyu is the other one.

00:14:59.820 --> 00:15:02.500
And Yagyu has a katana, which is awesome.

00:15:03.420 --> 00:15:13.860
They all have tradeoffs and they are the the I guess compensation for not having a live system is that they all have their own health bars.

00:15:13.860 --> 00:15:18.320
And when one dies, now that you have the others, you you go to them instead.

00:15:18.320 --> 00:15:26.720
Instead, I'd still much appreciate like some kind of, you know, life system so that it's not just over when everyone dies.

00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:39.800
But that said, I do like that they didn't just make them, you know, sprite changes and they actually like chin is slower, but he has more health and he has that double jump kick.

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:44.460
And Yagyu, as I said, starts with the sword and he has like a cool.

00:15:46.280 --> 00:15:50.100
Like front flip that gives him more jumping range.

00:15:50.280 --> 00:15:55.380
That's also useful in some of the later stages just for the platforming side of it.

00:15:56.140 --> 00:15:59.560
The names aren't quite as memorable as Billy and Jilly.

00:15:59.780 --> 00:16:00.700
I don't know. Jilly.

00:16:00.900 --> 00:16:01.760
I did it.

00:16:02.560 --> 00:16:05.040
I made my new version of Bimmy, Billy and Jimmy.

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:10.000
But, you know, chin and Yagyu were welcome additions to the roster.

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:17.740
I get that and I appreciate that they have a different play style as sort of not unlockable character.

00:16:17.860 --> 00:16:19.680
Yeah, they're unlockable characters basically.

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:31.320
But I don't think that they're a good replacement or stand in for lives because you then have to immediately adopt a different play style.

00:16:32.340 --> 00:16:34.540
And which it's not going to be radically different.

00:16:34.740 --> 00:16:35.960
It's still punch, kick, whatever.

00:16:35.960 --> 00:16:41.240
But you kind of have to modulate and just adjust.

00:16:41.620 --> 00:16:43.960
You have to think about the speed changes.

00:16:44.180 --> 00:16:52.400
And God forbid you end up in one of the actual platforming sections that usually these games don't have.

00:16:54.320 --> 00:17:00.420
But it's just a weird tradeoff that they made here that I don't think was...

00:17:00.420 --> 00:17:02.780
Like they didn't need to make that tradeoff.

00:17:03.940 --> 00:17:11.840
Yeah, I feel like that idea that you're describing, like it could work as a game mechanic but not in this game.

00:17:12.100 --> 00:17:15.220
Of like, oh, instead of a light, like you die, now you have to play the game a little differently.

00:17:15.300 --> 00:17:16.580
This game wasn't built around that.

00:17:16.720 --> 00:17:22.220
It doesn't feel like... I mean, even playing as chin to me doesn't feel like it was as...

00:17:22.220 --> 00:17:24.040
I don't know, it didn't feel like it fit as well.

00:17:24.040 --> 00:17:26.640
Like maybe it was just harder for me because I wasn't as used to it.

00:17:26.760 --> 00:17:34.200
But like after like just trying out playing as chin, I pretty much just went back to Billy until I unlocked Yagu.

00:17:34.460 --> 00:17:35.920
But like it...

00:17:35.920 --> 00:17:36.920
Yeah, I just...

00:17:36.920 --> 00:17:43.080
It like doesn't feel like there's much incentive for me to play as the other character.

00:17:43.180 --> 00:17:44.440
Maybe it's just because I have a preference.

00:17:44.760 --> 00:17:46.200
But yeah, that's how I felt.

00:17:46.620 --> 00:17:51.020
Yeah, the only preference really is like to exchange health, right?

00:17:51.180 --> 00:17:54.780
Like if you're low on one guy and don't want him to die, you would go to the next.

00:17:55.240 --> 00:18:02.740
But like they couldn't even have done something like, oh, you need to switch to this character to complete this section

00:18:02.740 --> 00:18:07.200
because then you would wind up having a bunch of softlock things if they had previously died.

00:18:07.200 --> 00:18:12.340
But do the different characters even make like sections notably easier?

00:18:12.740 --> 00:18:21.560
Like is anything like with the katana with Yagu, is that like any better than the...

00:18:21.560 --> 00:18:22.980
What do you get? You get the...

00:18:23.800 --> 00:18:29.620
You get the nunchucks with Billy and Jimmy and the iron claw with chin.

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:30.960
Like is that any different?

00:18:31.280 --> 00:18:32.380
I don't think so.

00:18:32.520 --> 00:18:33.700
I think they're just...

00:18:33.700 --> 00:18:36.400
It's just a more potent attack.

00:18:37.960 --> 00:18:39.320
And they're...

00:18:39.320 --> 00:18:40.380
They basically...

